.TH EUCLID-CLI-ESM-DELETE-OBJECTS 1 "September 2026" "euclid-cli 1.0.53" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-esm-delete-objects \- delete several objects from a bucket
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli esm delete-objects
.BR \-\-bucket | \-b
.I NAME
.RB [ \-\-keys | \-k
.IR LIST ]
.RB [ \-\-prefix | \-p
.IR KEY-PREFIX ]
.RB [ \-\-async ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Deletes objects from one bucket: the ones named by
.BR \-\-keys ,
or everything under
.BR \-\-prefix .
.PP
Each object's file, its row and one
.I esm.object.deleted
event go, exactly as a single-object delete would do it, so subscribers
see the same thing whichever way the object was removed.
.SH WHERE THIS SITS
.TP
.BR euclid-cli-esm-delete-object (1)
One object, addressed by its ERN. What an SDK calls, once per object.
.TP
.B delete-objects
Several objects of one bucket, named or by prefix. The case a caller
holding a list of keys used to have to loop over, one request each.
.TP
.BR euclid-cli-esm-purge-bucket (1)
Emptying a bucket, usually so it can be deleted. About the bucket rather
than about the objects.
.SH KEYS OR PREFIX, NOT BOTH
The two ask different questions \- "these" and "everything under there"
\- and answering both would delete more than either. Naming both is
refused rather than guessed at, because the mistake is not recoverable.
.PP
Naming neither deletes every object in the bucket. That is the same as
.BR euclid-cli-esm-purge-bucket (1)
and worth being sure about before pressing return.
.SH A MISSING KEY IS NOT AN ERROR
A key that names nothing is skipped, and the batch carries on. A list
assembled earlier should not fail wholesale because one object went in
the meantime, and "it is not there" is the outcome the caller asked for
anyway.
.PP
The answer reports both numbers, so a caller who cares can compare them:
.PP
.RS
.nf
{ "ern": "...:bucket:inbox", "asked": 12, "objects": 11 }
.fi
.RE
.SH LARGE DELETIONS
Removing an object is a file, a row and an event, and a long enough list
outlasts the request that asked for it \- the caller gets a timeout while
the removal carries on unseen behind the abandoned request.
.PP
.B \-\-async
answers immediately with
.B 202 Accepted
and the number of objects the request covers, and the removal is done by
a background thread inside ESM. The bucket's counters are adjusted when
it finishes, re-read at that point rather than assumed, since anything
written meanwhile is in the stored figure but not in what was removed.
.PP
Progress shows in
.BR euclid-cli-esm-get-bucket-size (1),
and ESM logs "background delete started" and "background delete
finished" with the count.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-b ", " \-\-bucket " " \fINAME\fR
Bucket to delete from. Required. A bucket name is enough; a full ERN
also works and is what reaches another namespace.
.TP
.BR \-k ", " \-\-keys " " \fILIST\fR
Comma-separated object keys. Cannot be combined with
.BR \-\-prefix .
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-prefix " " \fIKEY-PREFIX\fR
Delete everything whose key starts with this. Matched as a literal, not
a pattern. Cannot be combined with
.BR \-\-keys .
.TP
.B \-\-async
Answer at once and delete in the background.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The objects were deleted, or with
.B \-\-async
accepted for deletion. Counts are printed as JSON, including when
nothing matched.
.TP
.B 1
Missing arguments, both
.B \-\-keys
and
.B \-\-prefix
given, no session, the bucket does not exist, it belongs to another
account, the caller has no grant for it, or the server rejected the
request.
.SH EXAMPLES
Delete three objects by name:
.PP
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli esm delete-objects --bucket inbox \e
    --keys jvo/a.xml,jvo/b.xml,jvo/c.xml
.fi
.RE
.PP
Delete a directory's worth, without waiting:
.PP
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli esm delete-objects --bucket inbox \e
    --prefix jvo/incoming/ --async
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR euclid-cli-esm (1),
.BR euclid-cli-esm-delete-object (1),
.BR euclid-cli-esm-purge-bucket (1),
.BR euclid-cli-esm-list-objects (1)
.SH AUTHOR
Jens Vogt <jens.vogt@opitz-consulting.com>
